4 is MOSP,\nalthough vertex separation led in 2015--2019. Relevant works per five-year\nperiod, for the three names with the most works over 2005--2024; the next are\nedge search (28) and node search (20).\n\n--- sec3_names:80\nThe open-stacks papers are read apart from graph theory. Works citing\nthe papers that the Linhares--Yanasse table cites, by the discipline of the\npapers they cite. Of 135 works citing an operations-research paper, 6 also cite\na graph-theory paper (4\\%); of 285 citing a VLSI paper, 65 do (23\\%). The 2002\npaper itself counts as operations research. OpenAlex, 29 September 2026; 844\nciting works in all.\n\n--- sec4_search:124\nThe customer search is, ingredient by ingredient, an exact pathwidth\nalgorithm: each notion of the search beside its name in the pathwidth\nliterature. The memo and the definite move are described in\nSection~\\ref{sec:algorithm}.\n\n--- sec4_search:152\nThe customer search \\citep{ChuStuckey2009}. It decides $\\Sol_k(S)$\nfor a graph $G=(C,E)$ and a stack bound $k$, and is called as\n$\\textsc{Search}(\\emptyset,\\emptyset)$. $Q$ is the set of old moves the node\ninherits.\n\n--- sec4_search:246\nThe graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the state where the\ndefinite move fails. Customer 2 is closed; 1, 3 and 4 are open. Moving 0 opens\nthe new stacks 0, 7 and 11 and closes 0, 3 and 4, so open = close = 3 and the\nrule keeps 0 alone. But 3 and 4 have the single new stack 0 between them:\nclosing them first gains two stacks for the price of one, which a solution\nstarting with 0 cannot match.\n\n--- sec4_search:527\nTwo published rules are false; the free move, the memo and the\nrepaired definite move are known pathwidth results; the subset rule, the\nrepaired better move and the old move, like the matching test, are proved here.\nEach rule of the customer search against the exact-pathwidth literature.\n\n--- sec5_results:84\nThe repair never changed an answer and changes the total node count\nby at most 2\\%. Each pair runs the same question under the published and the\nrepaired rules in one worker. Totals are over the pairs that finished under\nboth rules; a pair stopped by its budget says nothing about cost.\n\\emph{Largest pair ratio}: the largest ratio of repaired to published nodes\nover single pairs. Open-stacks pairs refute the optimum minus one, at 600\\,s per\ncall at 50--100 customers and $2\\times10^8$ nodes at 125. The graph pairs run\nwhole descents on VSPLIB, the colouring and named graphs, and a sample of 500\nRome graphs.\n\n--- sec5_results:154\nEvery certificate the search emitted verifies, none is rejected, and\nchecking takes milliseconds. Certificates for the repaired rules, configuration\nwith better move, refuting the optimum minus one on the certified MOSP corpus\nat 9--75 customers, with 60\\,s to emit each. \\emph{Not emitted}: the emitter\ndid not finish in time. Size is the compressed certificate; check time is the\nindependent checker's.\n\n--- sec5_results:199\nTwenty collections, 21,754 instance files, reduce to 17,714\nisomorphism classes, and 16,087 of them have a certified pathwidth. The open\nclasses are mostly in the PACE sets, the VSPLIB grids and Harwell-Boeing\ngraphs, and the MOSP sets of Carvalho and Soma and of Frinhani et al. \\emph{Classes}: isomorphism classes within the\ncollection (78 graphs above 5,000 vertices are compared by file content only).\n\\emph{Counted here}: classes whose first member, in the order of the table,\nis in this collection, so the column sums to the dataset's class count. The\nnext four columns split those classes by provenance; \\emph{No witnessed\nvalue}: no layout is stored yet. $^{a}$The same 46 files distributed a second\ntime under other names; every class is counted under its first copy.\n\n--- sec9a_appendix_lean:40\nEach result of the paper and its formal statement in Lean.\n\n--- sec9b_appendix_machinery:169\nAll seven hardest values re-certify unchanged, at 18 to 369\ncore-hours each; the two values never certified are in flight, with no task\nsatisfiable so far (read 6 October 2026). \\emph{Value}: the stored optimum;\n\\emph{$k$}: the value minus one, which must be refuted. For an instance in\nflight, the nodes and core-hours are those of the tasks finished so far.\n\n--- sec9c_appendix_names:53\nWorks that use each problem's name in the sense of the\nLinhares--Yanasse table, and citations of the paper the table cites for it.\n\\emph{Relevant}: hits kept by the relevance check. \\emph{Citations}: OpenAlex\n\\texttt{cited\\_by\\_count}, 29 September 2026. Kashiwabara and Fujisawa (1979)\nhas no OpenAlex record. $^{a}$OpenAlex returned one pathwidth record twice: the\ncounts are of records, as Figure~\\ref{fig:names} draws them, and there are 1,212\ndistinct relevant pathwidth works.\n\n--- sec9c_appendix_names:76\nThe VLSI names belong to the 1980s, the search games peak later and\nfade, MOSP peaks in 2005--2014, and only pathwidth is still rising. The shade is\nthe name's rate, relevant works per million works in the same four fields in\neach five-year period, as a fraction of that name's own peak rate; the numbers\nare raw counts. Rows are ordered by the period of their peak. Counts cover\n1970--2024, since 2025--2026 is not fully indexed, so row totals are below those\nof Table~\\ref{tab:names}. Before 1990 (hatched) OpenAlex has few abstracts.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
